The vectors are drawn on a square grid, and $\mathbf{c}=\overrightarrow{OC}$.

![Vector diagram](images/e8/e83aea7ca6d1b1c0a13a6344c5299332cd42b0e67d9b9a34879bcc32015cf6c8.png)

Given that $\mathbf{c}=\lambda\mathbf{a}+\mu\mathbf{b}$, find $\lambda$ and $\mu$.

$\lambda=$ {{field-1}}

$\mu=$ {{field-2}}

question.:question/worked-solution:
![Completed vector route](images/1a/1a4d9465a9b11ccf5cf28d2dc28c45864a3c96fadb4d74130bb7381526a84ebe.png)

Construct the route $O\to X\to C$, first parallel to $\mathbf{b}$ and then parallel to $\mathbf{a}$.

Along $OX$, move $6$ squares left and $6$ squares down. A copy of $\mathbf{b}$ moves $2$ squares left and $2$ squares down. Thus $OX=3|\mathbf{b}|$ and the segment points in the same direction to $\mathbf{b}$. Hence $\overrightarrow{OX}=3\mathbf{b}$.

Along $XC$, move $4$ squares down. A copy of $\mathbf{a}$ moves $1$ square down. Thus $XC=4|\mathbf{a}|$ and the segment points in the same direction to $\mathbf{a}$. Hence $\overrightarrow{XC}=4\mathbf{a}$.

$$
\begin{aligned}
\mathbf{c}&=\overrightarrow{OX}+\overrightarrow{XC}\\&=3\mathbf{b}+4\mathbf{a}\\&=4\mathbf{a}+3\mathbf{b}
\end{aligned}
$$

Reorder the terms to match their vector names. Since $\mathbf{a}$ and $\mathbf{b}$ are not parallel, their coefficients are unique. Matching coefficients gives $\lambda=4$ and $\mu=3$.

