Along $OX$, move $2$ squares left. A copy of $\mathbf{b}$ moves $2$ squares left. Thus $OX=1|\mathbf{b}|$ and the segment points in the same direction to $\mathbf{b}$. Hence $\overrightarrow{OX}=\mathbf{b}$.

Along $XC$, move $1$ square left and $2$ squares down. A copy of $\mathbf{a}$ moves $1$ square left and $2$ squares down. Thus $XC=1|\mathbf{a}|$ and the segment points in the same direction to $\mathbf{a}$. Hence $\overrightarrow{XC}=\mathbf{a}$.

$$
\begin{aligned}
\mathbf{c}&=\overrightarrow{OX}+\overrightarrow{XC}\\&=\mathbf{b}+\mathbf{a}\\&=\mathbf{a}+\mathbf{b}
\end{aligned}
$$

Reorder the terms to match their vector names. Since $\mathbf{a}$ and $\mathbf{b}$ are not parallel, their coefficients are unique. Matching coefficients gives $\lambda=1$ and $\mu=1$.

